<h2>A small debt, a long procedural road</h2>
<p>The underlying claim was modest: a paper wholesaler sought about Rs 2.18 lakh in unpaid dues from the proprietor of a trading firm. The invoices carried a clause referring payment disputes to arbitration by an arbitrator appointed by the Executive Committee of the Paper Merchants Association (Regd.), Delhi. When a cheque issued towards the dues was not honoured, the Association&rsquo;s committee appointed a sole arbitrator. The buyer did not participate despite notices, and on 31 January 2020 the arbitrator awarded the principal with pre-award interest and further interest at 15% per annum until payment.</p>
<p>Execution is where the award met resistance. In objections, the award-debtor contended that he was not a member of the Association, had never consented in writing to the appointment after the dispute arose, and that the award was therefore a nullity rendered by a unilaterally appointed arbitrator. On 28 January 2025 the Commercial Court agreed, declared the appointment unilateral and the award a nullity, and dismissed the execution. The award-holder appealed to the Delhi High Court under the Commercial Courts Act.</p>

<h2>What the Division Bench held</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Independent body, not a party</h3><p>The arbitrator was chosen by the Association&rsquo;s Executive Committee, not by either litigant. The vice of a unilateral appointment is one party&rsquo;s control over the tribunal; that vice is absent where an outside body appoints under an agreed mechanism.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Membership is not bias</h3><p>An association&rsquo;s mechanism is not automatically neutral — but allegations of control or partiality need material showing the relationship. They cannot be presumed from the bare fact that one party belongs to the body.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Section 12(5) needs the Seventh Schedule</h3><p>The Commercial Court had identified no Seventh Schedule relationship between the arbitrator and the parties, counsel or subject-matter. Without established ineligibility, the question of a written waiver under the proviso never arises.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Consent is a question of conduct</h3><p>Invoice arbitration clauses can bind where the invoices were received and accepted in a continuing course of dealing — though a cheque against the debt does not, by itself, prove consent to arbitrate.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The execution court&rsquo;s narrow gate</h2>
<p>The judgment is as significant for what it says about execution as for what it says about appointments. An executing court generally cannot go behind the award; its task is enforcement, not review. But a genuine jurisdictional objection — one which, if made good, shows the award to be a nullity — can be examined even where the debtor never filed a Section 34 petition. The gate, however, is as narrow as the doctrine: the objection must establish nullity in law, not merely re-argue the dispute or raise grievances that belonged in a timely challenge. Here the only ground pressed was the supposed unilateral appointment, and once that failed on the Section 12(5) analysis, nothing remained. The Bench set aside the Commercial Court&rsquo;s order, rejected the appointment objection, and remitted the execution to proceed — without reopening the appointment issue, though other permissible objections, if any survive, may still be considered.</p>

<h2>Reading it with the unilateral-appointment case law</h2>
<p>Delhi&rsquo;s dockets have seen a steady run of awards felled by unilateral appointments — lenders naming their own arbitrators, clauses empowering one side&rsquo;s managing director, and panels controlled by one party. The Constitution Bench&rsquo;s intervention against unilateral appointments made the doctrine a powerful shield. This judgment marks its outer boundary. The doctrine protects the guarantee of an impartial tribunal; it is not a technicality that voids every appointment a respondent did not individually bless. Trade and commodity associations across Delhi — paper, textiles, grain, bullion — have long resolved member and non-member disputes through institutional appointment mechanisms, and the decision stabilises thousands of such awards against collateral attack at the execution stage.</p>

<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>For award-holders:</strong> plead and prove the course of dealing behind an invoice clause — received invoices, part payments, correspondence — rather than relying on the cheque alone.</li>
  <li><strong>For award-debtors:</strong> a Section 12(5) objection needs a named Seventh Schedule relationship. If the real grievance is the merits, the remedy was Section 34, within limitation — not objections years later in execution.</li>
  <li><strong>For associations:</strong> a transparent appointment process — a standing panel, disclosure by appointees, committee minutes — is the best insurance for the enforceability of awards rendered under institutional clauses.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Diarise the distinction when advising on enforcement risk. An appointment made <em>by a party</em> (or its employee or curated panel) remains presumptively vulnerable; an appointment made <em>by an institution or association under an agreed clause</em> is defensible unless the objector produces material of control or a Seventh Schedule link. The difference decides whether execution proceeds or the award unravels.</p>
</div>
